__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
build/
dist/
.venv/
venv/
# The run configurations of both IDEs are the commands in AGENTS.md written
# down where the IDE can run them, so they describe the project and are worth
# sharing. The two editors need opposite treatment to get there. PyCharm keeps
# its per-machine state in .idea beside those files, so everything there is
# ignored and only runConfigurations is let back through; the negation needs
# the /* on the line above it, because git will not descend into a directory
# it has already ignored. VS Code keeps per-machine state in the user profile
# instead, so .vscode holds project state by design and is not ignored at all.
.idea/*
!.idea/runConfigurations/
.pytest_cache/
.coverage
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# A lock file for a package with no dependencies pins nothing.
uv.lock

# Agent tooling scratch. A worktree is a checkout of this repository living
# inside it, which git would otherwise try to record as a nested repo, and
# settings.local.json is per-user. A shared .claude/settings.json is
# deliberately not ignored, so one can still be committed if ever wanted.
.claude/worktrees/
.claude/settings.local.json

# The per-tool pointer file that imports AGENTS.md. Kept out of the repository
# because it is specific to one agent, while the instructions it points at are
# written for any of them. Anyone who wants it writes a one line file holding
# @AGENTS.md and it is ignored from then on.
CLAUDE.md
